{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's analogy of 'betting on the horse with the best track record' inadvertently smuggles in induction by relying on the implicit premise that past performance (corroboration) is a reliable guide to future utility. Popper explicitly rejects the idea that surviving tests makes a theory more probable or reliable for the future; he only accepts it as not yet falsified. If a scientist chooses Theory A over Theory B because A is better corroborated, they are acting on the expectation that A will continue to perform well or not fail immediately, which is an inductive inference about the future based on the past. The target claims this is merely a 'pragmatic rule,' but fails to explain how one can pragmatically prefer X over Y without assuming X has a higher propensity for success, which collapses the distinction between corroboration and probability that Popper insisted upon.","premise":"Acting on a preference for a better-corroborated theory necessarily implies an expectation of its future reliability or success.","successor_question":"If corroboration provides no basis for expecting future success, on what rational grounds does Popper suggest we prefer the better-corroborated theory for practical action rather than flipping a coin?","target":"1e9dab1b64ea189edab5f8a13165944cbe7dc599d4cecdde2ed89adad1429c4f"}]}